  "latex_appendix": "\\paragraph{Domain Distribution in Extreme-BCS Batches.}\nWe classify the 80 FewRel relation types into six semantic domains (geography, creative work, person, organization, event/temporal, science/taxonomy) and compare domain composition between the 25 highest-BCS and 25 lowest-BCS batches from our 8-type schema pool.\nThe distributions differ significantly ($\\chi^2 = 230.74$, $p < 0.001$, $df = 5$): low-BCS batches draw heavily from geography (45.5\\%) and creative work (25.5\\%), while high-BCS batches concentrate on science/taxonomy (49.5\\%) and event/temporal (26.0\\%) domains.\nNormalized Shannon entropy is higher for low-BCS batches ($H' = 0.79$) than high-BCS ($H' = 0.67$), confirming that low-BCS schemas span more diverse domains.\nCrucially, this domain concentration is an \\emph{expected} property of embedding-based confusability: relation types within the same domain naturally share semantic features, producing higher pairwise cosine similarity and thus higher BCS.\nDomain composition therefore acts as a mediator---it is \\emph{part of} the mechanism through which BCS captures schema difficulty---rather than an independent confounding variable.\nThe BCS metric intentionally aggregates such semantic overlap into a single predictive score."
